    "text": "The Herth Hope Index (HHI) yields a total score ranging from 12 to 84, with higher scores indicating greater levels of hope, as well as three subscale scores ranging from 4 to 28:\nSense of temporality and future (Items 1, 2, 6 [reverse-scored], and 11): Assesses cognitive-temporal aspects of hope, including belief that each day has potential, maintaining a positive outlook, having short and long-term goals, and experiencing confidence about the future. Higher scores indicate stronger future orientation and goal-directed thinking.\nPositive readiness and expectancy (Items 4, 7, 10, and 12): Measures affective-behavioural aspects of hope, including the ability to see possibilities in difficult situations, sense of direction, recognition of life’s value and worth, and capacity to recall happy memories. Higher scores suggest greater emotional resilience and readiness to act on hope.\nInterconnectedness with self and others (Items 3 [reverse-scored], 5, 8, and 9): Evaluates affiliative-contextual aspects of hope, including feelings of connection versus isolation, presence of comforting or spiritual beliefs, inner strength, and ability to give and receive care and love. Higher scores indicate stronger interpersonal connections and beliefs that sustain hope.\n\n\n\n",
    "source_url": null
   }
  ],
  "reportScoringGuidance": [
   {
    "documentSha256": "72607fd649e5a7ede8f3d837156f752f517aca2d20299c1e931b2c0874f47620",
    "pageNumber": 2,
    "text": "For comprehensive information on the HHI, \nsee here\n.\nThe Herth Hope Index (HHI) yields a total score ranging from 12 to 84, with higher scores\nindicating greater levels of hope, as well as three subscale scores ranging from 4 to 28:\n- Sense of temporality and future (Items 1, 2, 6 [reverse-scored], and 11): Assesses cognitive-\ntemporal aspects of hope, including belief that each day has potential, maintaining a positive\noutlook, having short and long-term goals, and experiencing confidence about the future. Higher\nscores indicate stronger future orientation and goal-directed thinking.\n- Positive readiness and expectancy (Items 4, 7, 10, and 12): Measures affective-behavioural\naspects of hope, including the ability to see possibilities in difficult situations, sense of direction,\nrecognition of life's value and worth, and capacity to recall happy memories. Higher scores\nsuggest greater emotional resilience and readiness to act on hope.\n- Interconnectedness with self and others (Items 3 [reverse-scored], 5, 8, and 9): Evaluates\naffiliative-contextual aspects of hope, including feelings of connection versus isolation, presence\nof comforting or spiritual beliefs, inner strength, and ability to give and receive care and love.\nHigher scores indicate stronger interpersonal connections and beliefs that sustain hope.",
